WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.305 --> 00:00:02.603
- So you know, a big
part of what I focus on

00:00:02.603 --> 00:00:06.261
at any given time is making
sure we are innovating

00:00:06.261 --> 00:00:07.862
and building products for the future.

00:00:07.862 --> 00:00:12.253
One brilliant invention can
unleash other entrepreneurs

00:00:12.253 --> 00:00:15.301
to revolutionize industries in
ways you could never predict.

00:00:15.301 --> 00:00:17.907
We want to push the technology at times

00:00:17.907 --> 00:00:20.219
because you don't know what's
possible on the other side.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:22.435
You have to work with people where

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:23.972
you feel a bit insecure.

00:00:23.972 --> 00:00:25.362
That's essential, because that means

00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:28.071
you're working with people
who are better than you.

00:00:28.071 --> 00:00:30.687
- He's an Indian-American
business executive.

00:00:30.687 --> 00:00:32.720
He's the current CEO of Google.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.482
He has an estimated net
worth of one billion dollars.

00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:37.707
He's Sundar Pichai and
here's my take on his

00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:39.456
top ten rules for success.

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:41.222
Rule number seven is my personal favorite

00:00:41.222 --> 00:00:43.091
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:44.014
you guys like the best.

00:00:44.014 --> 00:00:45.758
Also as you're watching,
if he says something that

00:00:45.758 --> 00:00:48.379
really hits you hard,
really means a lot to you,

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:50.141
leave it in the comments
and put quotes around it

00:00:50.141 --> 00:00:53.474
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:57.657
- From a perspective,
and I think it's true for

00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:00.484
technology in particular, you
know the world keeps changing

00:01:00.484 --> 00:01:03.938
as I said earlier, so a
big part of what I focus on

00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:07.801
at any given time is making
sure we are innovating

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.485
and building products for the future.

00:01:09.485 --> 00:01:13.035
It's just got to be a normal
course of how you think.

00:01:13.035 --> 00:01:15.832
And so we are constantly thinking about

00:01:15.832 --> 00:01:17.659
what to do next.

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:19.201
You know, Android is very popular,

00:01:19.201 --> 00:01:22.286
people are using smartphones,
but I always sit and

00:01:22.286 --> 00:01:23.784
think about what is
the next version of how

00:01:23.784 --> 00:01:25.559
people will use computing?

00:01:25.559 --> 00:01:29.028
So we are thinking about
things like virtual reality

00:01:29.028 --> 00:01:31.272
or augmented reality, so
these are all new areas,

00:01:31.272 --> 00:01:32.926
but we are constantly thinking about it.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:37.145
So you have to do that on a
constant basis to push forward.

00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:39.392
The thing which attracted me to Google

00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:43.559
and to internet in general is
that it's a great equalizer.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.953
So to me, I've always been
struck by the fact that

00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:54.098
Google search worked the
same, as long as you had

00:01:54.098 --> 00:01:56.125
access to a computer with connectivity,

00:01:56.125 --> 00:01:58.349
if you were a rural kid
anywhere or if you were

00:01:58.349 --> 00:02:01.546
a professor at Stanford or Harvard.

00:02:01.546 --> 00:02:05.638
To me, I want Google to
strive to push to do that.

00:02:05.638 --> 00:02:09.997
Not just build technology
for certain segments, right?

00:02:09.997 --> 00:02:14.065
For me it matters that we drive technology

00:02:14.065 --> 00:02:17.837
as an equalizing force, as an enabler to

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.531
everyone around the world.

00:02:19.531 --> 00:02:22.449
Growing up in India, like
many of you, I got my first

00:02:22.449 --> 00:02:24.524
telephone when I was 12.

00:02:24.524 --> 00:02:27.454
In my case, it turned
out to be a rotary phone,

00:02:27.454 --> 00:02:29.596
so it wasn't that great for selfies.

00:02:29.596 --> 00:02:30.429
(audience laughs)

00:02:30.429 --> 00:02:33.191
But I still loved to call
my friends, play with it,

00:02:33.191 --> 00:02:35.321
and sometimes take it apart.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:39.290
That telephone cemented my
fascination with technology.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.029
I remember in my parents'
house in Chennai,

00:02:42.029 --> 00:02:45.508
reading about the invention of
the transistor at Bell Labs.

00:02:45.508 --> 00:02:49.050
Of course, that initial
invention helped found

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.201
what became known as Silicon
Valley, and out of that

00:02:52.201 --> 00:02:55.924
came companies like Fairchild
Semiconductor and Intel,

00:02:55.924 --> 00:02:57.910
and all the computers and software

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.719
that we all use today.

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:02.669
You can draw a direct
line from that invention

00:03:02.669 --> 00:03:05.480
to the technology that
powers your Twitter feed

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.083
or your ReChat messages today.

00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:10.547
I remember reading
about that and thinking,

00:03:10.547 --> 00:03:12.582
"It's the idea that matters."

00:03:12.582 --> 00:03:14.492
It didn't matter where you come from,

00:03:14.492 --> 00:03:16.446
or what your background is.

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:20.302
One revolutionary idea,
one brilliant invention,

00:03:20.302 --> 00:03:24.189
can unleash other entrepreneurs
to revolutionize industries

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:26.443
in ways you could never predict.

00:03:26.443 --> 00:03:30.610
You will have many many
opportunities to reinvent yourself.

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:36.256
So I think it's worthwhile taking risks

00:03:36.256 --> 00:03:40.022
and trying to do something
you're really excited by.

00:03:40.022 --> 00:03:41.675
If at the first attempt
you don't do it, you know,

00:03:41.675 --> 00:03:43.375
you can try again and things tend to

00:03:43.375 --> 00:03:44.958
work out in the long run.

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:48.019
In Silicon Valley, part of
the reason so many people

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:51.452
start up a company is,
starting up a company

00:03:51.452 --> 00:03:53.286
and even having failed,
you can wear it like

00:03:53.286 --> 00:03:55.478
a badge of honor, right?

00:03:55.478 --> 00:04:00.051
I think that's important,
culturally, risk is rewarded.

00:04:00.051 --> 00:04:02.323
I remember when I started
working at Google,

00:04:02.323 --> 00:04:05.896
if I went and people
were discussing ideas,

00:04:05.896 --> 00:04:07.712
the other people who
heard the ideas try to

00:04:07.712 --> 00:04:10.281
build on those ideas, they encourage you.

00:04:10.281 --> 00:04:12.815
So it's a culture of optimism,
it's a culture of risk taking

00:04:12.815 --> 00:04:14.883
and I think that's really important.

00:04:14.883 --> 00:04:17.098
- Are there any Jedi mind
tricks that you're employing

00:04:17.098 --> 00:04:20.349
that, you know, maybe
people aren't seeing?

00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:22.495
That you're doing to
motivate your employees?

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.762
- You know for me, it
mainly is, I've always been

00:04:24.762 --> 00:04:27.914
very passionate, so I'm an optimist about

00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:30.031
how technology can make a difference.

00:04:30.031 --> 00:04:32.995
So I focus on that, and
that way you kind of tend to

00:04:32.995 --> 00:04:35.852
forget about the other things.

00:04:35.852 --> 00:04:38.214
The rest is noise in some ways.

00:04:38.214 --> 00:04:41.145
We believe that software
is at a stage where

00:04:41.145 --> 00:04:43.831
software increasingly is
playing a more and more

00:04:43.831 --> 00:04:46.895
critical role in solving
things, which it didn't before.

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:47.728
- Okay.

00:04:47.728 --> 00:04:49.315
- So to me when I look at cars,

00:04:49.315 --> 00:04:52.722
people spend an inordinate
amount of time in cars.

00:04:52.722 --> 00:04:55.467
These are resources which
are very poorly utilized.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:58.398
Right now as we speak,
you can look outside

00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:00.641
and you can see all the
cars which are parked.

00:05:00.641 --> 00:05:02.019
- Right, the car that
we rented to get here

00:05:02.019 --> 00:05:03.149
is just sitting in the parking lot.

00:05:03.149 --> 00:05:05.702
- That's right, so they
get used less than 10%.

00:05:05.702 --> 00:05:07.312
So we see these problems, and we say okay,

00:05:07.312 --> 00:05:09.523
can we solve it at
scale, and does computing

00:05:09.523 --> 00:05:12.334
play a part in it, software
and computer science?

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:16.961
And while the effort may
seem ambitious or crazy,

00:05:16.961 --> 00:05:19.443
we take a very disciplined
approach inside.

00:05:19.443 --> 00:05:23.110
So those are thought
through like businesses

00:05:23.983 --> 00:05:25.803
which we are building.

00:05:25.803 --> 00:05:28.740
It's just that we are willing
to take a long term view,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.728
but we run them in a very disciplined way.

00:05:31.728 --> 00:05:34.675
Our research can be longer
term, and we do that

00:05:34.675 --> 00:05:38.569
precisely in research when
we take research projects

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:41.248
like Google IE tab or our core research,

00:05:41.248 --> 00:05:44.220
we never know whether
some of them even makes

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.136
viable business
applications, but we want to

00:05:46.136 --> 00:05:49.084
push the technology at
times, because you don't know

00:05:49.084 --> 00:05:51.124
what's possible on the other side.

00:05:51.124 --> 00:05:54.231
Being in the U.S., I find people take a

00:05:54.231 --> 00:05:57.884
remarkably varied number of paths.

00:05:57.884 --> 00:06:00.405
So I do think it's important
to follow your dream

00:06:00.405 --> 00:06:03.905
and do something which you are excited by.

00:06:04.862 --> 00:06:07.696
I think if you follow your
heart and do what you like,

00:06:07.696 --> 00:06:09.524
you will always do much better.

00:06:09.524 --> 00:06:12.560
I don't think it matters
that you're an engineer or

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.004
you're in science, or
it can be in any field.

00:06:17.004 --> 00:06:18.261
- What is your morning routine like?

00:06:18.261 --> 00:06:19.512
What do you do when you
first get up in the morning?

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:21.873
- Believe it or not I still,
I read a physical paper

00:06:21.873 --> 00:06:24.044
every single morning.
- Which one?

00:06:24.044 --> 00:06:26.300
- I read the Wall Street
Journal every morning.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.349
I read the New York Times online.

00:06:28.349 --> 00:06:32.902
And I still am very particular
about having my tea,

00:06:32.902 --> 00:06:35.198
it's very English, you
know I grew up in India,

00:06:35.198 --> 00:06:36.615
so having my tea.

00:06:38.041 --> 00:06:40.131
I'm vegetarian, so I
need to get my protein

00:06:40.131 --> 00:06:44.692
so I always have an omelet in
the morning with toast, tea,

00:06:44.692 --> 00:06:47.258
and read my paper, so I
do that every morning.

00:06:47.258 --> 00:06:48.863
- What time do you wake up?

00:06:48.863 --> 00:06:51.285
- 6:30 to seven, so that's when I do it.

00:06:51.285 --> 00:06:53.834
- Do you exercise in the morning?

00:06:53.834 --> 00:06:57.064
- No, later in the day
sometimes if I get a chance,

00:06:57.064 --> 00:06:58.432
but always in the evening.

00:06:58.432 --> 00:07:00.723
I wish I could do it in
the morning, I'm not a

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:03.477
morning person so I need my
time with my paper and tea

00:07:03.477 --> 00:07:05.973
to wake up and kind of get going.

00:07:05.973 --> 00:07:07.865
- Now you're being spoken
about as a potential

00:07:07.865 --> 00:07:09.322
next CEO of Google.

00:07:09.322 --> 00:07:12.320
- (laughs) I keep hearing
that, I mean Larry is

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.489
very very committed to Google
for the very long term,

00:07:14.489 --> 00:07:17.651
so am I, I'm passionate about computing.

00:07:17.651 --> 00:07:19.997
Google gives a chance
for me to do what I love

00:07:19.997 --> 00:07:23.701
and do it in a way which
impacts a lot of people.

00:07:23.701 --> 00:07:25.899
So it's a good partnership
I have with Larry

00:07:25.899 --> 00:07:29.553
and with the team there, so
I expect it to stay the same.

00:07:29.553 --> 00:07:31.692
You know I would actually
encourage all of you,

00:07:31.692 --> 00:07:35.750
if at some point in your life
you have to work with people

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.699
where you feel a bit insecure.

00:07:37.699 --> 00:07:39.440
That's essential, because
that means you're working with

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:44.137
people who are better than
you, and who are pushing you.

00:07:44.137 --> 00:07:46.663
So I will always encourage
you, if you actually feel

00:07:46.663 --> 00:07:49.269
very secure in what you
do, that means you're

00:07:49.269 --> 00:07:51.685
doing something comfortable
and not pushing yourself.

00:07:51.685 --> 00:07:54.596
So there are many many times I've felt,

00:07:54.596 --> 00:07:57.012
working with people and a
group, am I doing enough?

00:07:57.012 --> 00:07:58.903
These people seem much better than me.

00:07:58.903 --> 00:08:02.117
I think that's an
inherent part of learning.

00:08:02.117 --> 00:08:03.458
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:08:03.458 --> 00:08:06.737
I made this video because
Yash Chavan asked me to.

00:08:06.737 --> 00:08:08.729
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:10.932
to profile next, leave it
down in the comments below

00:08:10.932 --> 00:08:12.142
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:12.142 --> 00:08:14.086
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:08:14.086 --> 00:08:16.264
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:08:16.264 --> 00:08:18.747
Leave it in the comments, I'm
going to join in the discussion.

00:08:18.747 --> 00:08:20.582
Finally I wanted to give
a quick shout out to

00:08:20.582 --> 00:08:22.862
Jonathan Chew from chewsjoy.com,

00:08:22.862 --> 00:08:24.657
Jonathan thank you so
much for buying my book,

00:08:24.657 --> 00:08:26.110
it really means a lot to me.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:27.677
For those of you watching,
if you want your chance

00:08:27.677 --> 00:08:30.109
at a shout out in a
future video, make sure to

00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:32.425
pick up a copy of the book
and email in your receipt

00:08:32.425 --> 00:08:33.403
so we can keep track.

00:08:33.403 --> 00:08:35.211
Than you guys so much for watching.

00:08:35.211 --> 00:08:37.920
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is,

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.837
and I'll see you soon.

